WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.080
- I don't know how to run a business

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.750
that needs to perform
financially every 90 days

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.203
because I'm a marathon
runner, not a sprinter.

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:10.410
(shouting)

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.903
You got your perspective.

00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:15.308
(cheering)

00:00:15.308 --> 00:00:16.141
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:17.320
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:18.207 --> 00:00:20.820
(jazzy piano music)

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.840
- Hello everyone, I'm Andy Serwer.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.450
Welcome to Influencers,

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.880
and welcome to our guest, Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.713
Gary, great to see you.

00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:28.620
- Great to see you.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:29.730
- Although, I should say our host,

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:30.563
because we're here,

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:31.900
at your building in Long Island City,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:32.940
so thank you for having us here.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.130
- We're thrilled to have you.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.340
- So let me ask you this
very basic question.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.173
- Sure.

00:00:37.173 --> 00:00:38.770
- How would you describe yourself?

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.860
Say, my six-year old nephew.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.398
What do you do?

00:00:42.398 --> 00:00:43.643
- I'm an entrepreneur,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.130
at its most purest sense.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.160
I am an operating-entrepreneur,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.940
who happens to put out a
lot of personal content

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.680
of that journey,

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.690
so, there is some awareness
of who I am as a human,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.140
but at the end of the day,

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.470
I'm an executive of

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.510
several companies that I run,

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.530
VaynerMedia and the
VaynerX holding company

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.900
of agencies and
service-providing companies

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.793
is really, really, really what I do.

00:01:15.498 --> 00:01:16.380
And that's it.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.740
It's not super-complicated

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.180
other than the fact, that I decided

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.120
that it was in our business interest

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.930
and in my own curiosity
of what would it be like

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.420
if you documented and produced content

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.040
at a scale that had never been seen before

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.970
around an executive.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.490
What would it have looked
like, if Ariana Huffington

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.410
when she started the Huffington Post,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.710
what if she put out five to
10 pieces of content a day,

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.340
in podcasts, vlog, and content

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.830
across all those social platforms,

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.540
every day of the first
six years of that journey?

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:49.490
What would that look like,

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.620
and that's what I decided to do.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.350
- I mean, in a way, it's kind of timeless,

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.080
like the entrepreneur part,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.860
on the other hand, you
couldn't do what you do today,

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.250
say, 20 years ago.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.700
- Not only that, I didn't
do, what I do today,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.570
when I ran my first company.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:07.893
- When I took the reins of my
dad's liquor store in 1998.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:11.560
The cynical part of being so out-and-about

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.430
is the showmanship, the
narcissism, the self-promotion.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.280
I try to remind people, I'm like, "Look...

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.030
A lot of times the accolades
that I get in business today

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.027
people pass-off as,

00:02:23.027 --> 00:02:25.230
"Well he's internet-famous and
that's why it's happening."

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.350
So, it undermines my
operational and executive skills

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.360
and when I get sad about that in a debate

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.620
or a conversation with a friend or a foe,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:33.947
I try to remind them like,

00:02:33.947 --> 00:02:36.487
"Look, I was a 22-year old kid,

00:02:36.487 --> 00:02:38.367
"who from 22

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:39.927
"to 28,

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:43.767
"grew a business from 3 million
to 60 million dollars a year

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:45.347
"and I didn't make content.

00:02:45.347 --> 00:02:46.787
"I wasn't on the internet.

00:02:46.787 --> 00:02:48.117
"I was an operator.

00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:49.320
"I was a marketer."

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.660
And so, yes, you're absolutely right.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.380
Not only could other people
have not done it in the past,

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.520
I already lived an entire chapter

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.560
of building an entire business

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.230
that had nothing to do
with having a personality

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.290
attached to it.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:03.960
I was just the operator of.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.070
- Yeah, it's pre-internet.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.450
And yeah, you needed your social skills,

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.260
but you were running an
operating-business as you said.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.093
But let me ask you,

00:03:11.093 --> 00:03:13.787
you've got 5.5 million
followers on Instagram,

00:03:13.787 --> 00:03:15.990
1.8 million on Twitter.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:16.823
How do you do that?

00:03:16.823 --> 00:03:17.677
I mean, I always hear people,

00:03:17.677 --> 00:03:19.940
"Oh, you just create
content and you sit there."

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.600
But it couldn't possibly be that easy.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.270
- Well, it's definitely not that easy.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.737
That would be like saying,

00:03:26.737 --> 00:03:29.060
"How do you become a
professional basketball player?"

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.800
You just play basketball.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.550
Like how do you become...
- Do you need talent then?

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:33.383
- Yes.

00:03:33.383 --> 00:03:34.750
- So what's the talent?
- By the way,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.330
let's stay here for four seconds,

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.500
because this is a huge platform,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.190
and I think we're about
to help a lot of people.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:43.040
The number one non-conversation
going on in today's society

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.510
around influencers and other things

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.988
is the talent part.

00:03:46.988 --> 00:03:48.130
- Mm-hmm, right.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:51.030
- Now, talent comes in
all shapes and sizes.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.030
Your talent may be that you're a model.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.620
You're just very attractive.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:55.453
- That's me.
- Right?

00:03:55.453 --> 00:03:56.286
- You know that.
- No, that's not me.

00:03:56.286 --> 00:03:57.119
- Right?
- Yes.

00:03:57.119 --> 00:04:00.030
Or a comedian or an athlete,
or a thought-leader.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.350
I do believe that
education and entertainment

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.563
are the two pillars,

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.160
but yes,

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.470
back in 2006,

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:10.570
I started making wine videos on YouTube.

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.860
In 2007, I aggressively
put out content on Twitter.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.940
Had the things coming out of my mouth,

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.750
not been interesting to people,

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.690
I would not be sitting
here with you right now.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.300
It's not super-complicated.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.480
Most people have taken
some version of an attempt

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.440
to become, somebody, people
know on the internet.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.307
Most people.

00:04:30.307 --> 00:04:32.580
You know there's a set
that's very private,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.210
most people have taken a dabble

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:36.830
into a LinkedIn article,

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.690
into a nice photograph
they posted on Instagram,

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.960
into a single YouTube video,

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.140
a couple clever tweets,

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.080
most people have tried.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:46.913
- Right.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.250
So what's the secret

00:04:50.215 --> 00:04:52.370
of making people engage with your content?

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.020
I mean it's just smarts?
- Empathy.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.330
- [Andy] Empathy, okay.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.540
- Empathy's the secret.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.100
I believe, I sit

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.190
at where I am in the Lexicon,

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.070
which is, I think,

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:03.750
a winning player in
today's new environment

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.690
because I'm empathetic to my audience.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.700
I put out content, that brings them value,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.710
not, makes me feel good about myself.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.543
- Right.

00:05:12.543 --> 00:05:15.397
How many posts do you put up
a day in Insta and Twitter?

00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:17.600
And those are your two main platforms?

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:18.790
- Those are the two main platforms

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.693
that I myself completely control.

00:05:21.693 --> 00:05:24.300
LinkedIn, Facebook, YouTube,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.130
I've over the last three or four years

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.420
amassed a team of content-creators,

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.060
media-amplifiers, strategists
around my content,

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:36.230
but I fully control Instagram and Twitter

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:39.460
and I put out four Instagram,

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.220
regular posts
(train horn sounding)

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.160
and probably,

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:46.963
anywhere between three
and 25 tweets a day.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.370
- So, that's a lot.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:52.490
- By the way, big shout
out to you and your team,

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.510
because the train going by now,

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.053
the fact that you guys didn't stop it.

00:05:56.053 --> 00:05:57.430
I am so pumped right now.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:59.030
I'm fired up.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.480
- We wouldn't stop for that.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.080
If my phone rang we wouldn't stop.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:03.990
- I love that.
- That's like old people,

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:04.880
old school stuff.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.970
- But there is an evolution
in content creation.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.010
Like it literally just took note

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:09.230
I'm like, "Oh, I like these...

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:10.063
good job.
- Good.

00:06:10.063 --> 00:06:10.896
- Go.

00:06:10.896 --> 00:06:12.770
- And that's empathetic.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.330
So, how does the business model work?

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.160
So, explain your company to me.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.340
Is it VaynerMedia,

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:19.530
is that the entire business--

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.810
- VaynerMedia was the first company

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.240
for about six or seven years--
- You know, but look

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.220
this is Vayner water--
- Yeah, I mean that's

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.930
just inside stuff, yeah, yeah.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:27.190
That's an inside stuff for us.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:28.023
We have fun with that

00:06:28.023 --> 00:06:30.380
but VaynerMedia was the first company,

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:31.870
after building up Wine Library

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.890
and then, after being an investor

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:35.090
in Facebook, Twitter, Tumblr,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:37.590
and having kind of Silicon Valley success,

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:41.070
I decided to start the
agency with the premise that,

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:45.610
eventually, I would wanna buy
nostalgic and historic brands.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.340
And I would run them through my

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.980
progressive, consumer-centric
marketing model.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.203
So, I started an agency.

00:06:53.868 --> 00:06:55.600
It grew very quickly,

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.570
now there's something called VaynerX

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:00.440
because two years ago
I bought PureWow.com,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.260
a women's lifestyle brand,

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:05.440
we started a men's brand
recently, called ONE37pm.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.810
Then we started a speaking
bureau six months ago

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.300
called VaynerSpeakers.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.630
We started a company in January

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:12.980
to service small businesses

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.210
'cause VaynerMedia's
made for Fortune 500s,

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.060
called the Sasha Group, named
after my dad, which is fun.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.060
We have a tech platform called Tracer

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.170
that is evolving in a separate company,

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:25.100
so, now there's a VaynerX holding company,

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.660
and very honestly,

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:30.490
it's really no different than
Publicis or Omnicon or WPP,

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.810
or, for people who are watching,

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.100
it's just Mad Men, 20/20, right?

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.290
Obviously, a hell of a lot.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.210
I haven't watched the show

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.390
but I assume, what
they've had on the show,

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:42.640
it's a heck of a lot culturally better.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.667
- Right, right, right, right.

00:07:44.667 --> 00:07:45.500
- It's an advertising agency.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.730
- Right, so the business model works

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.940
like, you have Fortune 500 clients,

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:50.270
like, give me some names.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.530
- Chase, Budweiser, Pepsi, Hulu.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.980
- And they pay you money--
- Service fees.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.600
- To make campaigns?
- Make pictures,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.250
and videos on the internet
and on television.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.560
Planters' Super Bowl spot this year

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:06.170
was made by this production
company that we're in

00:08:07.025 --> 00:08:09.640
and ideated creatively by VaynerMedia.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.050
We are paid to make pictures,

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:14.920
videos, written words,
marketing collateral.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.300
What's unique about VaynerMedia,
in today's world is,

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.320
under the same roof, we also
then are the media agency

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:21.970
that places the media.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:25.530
We buy the ads on Facebook,
Twitter, YouTube, Yahoo,

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.060
we do the execution.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.570
That was the way it was
done in the 60s and 70s.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.890
The same people that made it,
also bought the television.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:33.930
Then they separated them

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.940
and now most agencies
either do creative or media.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:38.260
VaynerMedia does both.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.783
- I mean a lot of people
say that ad-businesses,

00:08:40.783 --> 00:08:42.300
the legacy companies,

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.830
I mean certainly they're
under a lot of pressure,

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:45.040
Is this sort of a new model,

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.410
a good way to do things?
- I think so.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.660
Yeah, I agree

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.580
with the street and the
business pontificators

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.140
that the old model's in trouble

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.590
but I don't think it's necessarily

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.160
because they didn't have media
and creative under one roof.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.290
I believe it's because they
were publicly-traded companies,

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.930
and they had to make their own numbers,

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.220
and when you're a service provider,

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:10.930
if you only care about your
EBDA and your stock price,

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:13.540
your clients, eventually
are gonna feel that.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:14.690
And when they feel that

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:17.130
and they feel the negative effects...

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.210
I actually believe there's an
incredible book to be written

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.350
similar to "Barbarians at the Gate",

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:24.470
of what the four or five
biggest holding companies

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.060
in the world have accomplished
in the last 25 years,

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.713
which is through consolidation,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.740
they have basically extracted the value

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:34.130
out of the biggest brands in the world.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:35.470
If you look at the biggest
brands in the world

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.050
that have used the
publicly-traded agency model

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.040
over the last 20 years,

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.710
they've declined,

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.980
while the agencies have grown.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:45.190
Only now, are they starting to decline

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.820
because the cat's out of the bag

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:47.990
'cause the biggest brands in the world

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:49.130
are starting to look at Vayner

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.800
and other independent alternatives

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.100
and that's bad for the holding companies.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.603
- So, you wouldn't
necessarily look to go public?

00:09:56.603 --> 00:10:00.310
- I'm incapable of being a CEO
of a publicly-traded company.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:01.530
So, no, we will not.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:03.110
- Yeah, maybe Elon Musk

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.050
should have said something
like that at one point.

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.110
- I don't know Elon well enough

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.140
and I know he's a character.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.300
I'm not incapable because
I wanna curse on video.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:13.444
I'm incapable because I don't
know how to run a business

00:10:13.444 --> 00:10:17.240
that needs to perform
financially, every 90 days

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.500
because I'm a marathon
runner, not a sprinter.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.186
- That's kind of refreshing to hear that,

00:10:22.186 --> 00:10:23.287
you don't necessarily need this payout.

00:10:23.287 --> 00:10:25.290
But you don't need that
for your employees,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.344
I mean, there are a lot
of private companies

00:10:27.344 --> 00:10:29.230
that have been around for
decades and decades, right?

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.063
- That's exactly right.

00:10:30.063 --> 00:10:31.220
- You don't have to go public, right?

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:35.070
- No, I don't want the cash
infusion to buy a boat.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:37.290
I want to enjoy running a company

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:40.640
and I need independence
as an entrepreneur.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:41.563
And so,

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:45.790
I'm not willing to give up my independence

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.140
for 400 million dollars up front.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:49.629
- So, you were running a liquor store--

00:10:49.629 --> 00:10:50.462
- Yes.

00:10:50.462 --> 00:10:51.430
- And then, the internet happens.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:52.810
How and when did you realize

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:55.260
that the internet was gonna revolutionize,

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.270
let's just say, the business of

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.060
communication--
- October 1994.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:00.893
- You have a date?

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:02.250
- I do, I'll tell you why.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.300
October of 1994 was

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:06.930
the October of my
freshman year of college.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:08.280
And...

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.020
In 1991, when I was in high school,

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:12.480
that's when I started becoming passionate

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.313
about my dad's liquor store,

00:11:13.313 --> 00:11:16.157
and I said, "I'm gonna open 150 of these.

00:11:16.157 --> 00:11:18.170
"I'm gonna open the Toys
"R" Us of liquor stores."

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:19.970
I remember being a high
school kid thinking.

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:22.230
And then, in October of 1994,

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.640
I went into my friend's dorm room

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:24.850
and discovered the internet.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:26.480
And within 10 minutes,

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:30.480
I found myself on a baseball
card bulletin-board on AOL,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.290
and I saw that people were selling.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:34.234
And actually,

00:11:34.234 --> 00:11:35.067
- There we go.

00:11:35.067 --> 00:11:35.900
- They're not gonna see it,
but you're gonna see it.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:37.070
- Where?
- These are literally--

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:37.973
goosebumps--
- Goosebumps,

00:11:37.973 --> 00:11:38.806
that's goosebumps.
- That's right.

00:11:38.806 --> 00:11:39.730
This is real goosebumps,

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:40.580
and I'll tell you why,

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:41.763
it changed my life.

00:11:42.765 --> 00:11:45.250
I mean I'm really getting them right now.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.083
- That's legit.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:48.600
- So, I'm excited about this

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.206
'cause it was a really
transcending moment.

00:11:51.206 --> 00:11:52.039
I'm a weird kid,

00:11:52.039 --> 00:11:54.500
meaning I was a true entrepreneur.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.400
- '94 was early but that
was before Netscape.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.290
Way before Netscape.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:11:59.410
- I'm not sure the timing--

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:00.490
- Yeah, yeah, but it was.
- Is that right?

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:01.452
I believe you.

00:12:01.452 --> 00:12:02.285
- 'Cause you were on
an AOL bulletin board.

00:12:02.285 --> 00:12:03.118
- Yep, yep.

00:12:03.118 --> 00:12:05.320
And I just remember thinking

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.390
this is it.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:07.350
I don't know what else to say.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.183
I was like this is it,

00:12:08.183 --> 00:12:11.100
and literally two years later,

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:12.650
'cause it took a little bit of time,

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:14.430
I launched winelibrary.com,

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:17.120
one of the first e-commerce
wine businesses in America

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:20.300
and it changed the course of
my family's wine business.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.003
- So how do you build an online brand?

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:25.810
- Easily.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.840
You produce content

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.273
on the seven to 25 places
where people pay attention,

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:35.950
and you produce it around things you know,

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:38.795
and you amass awareness.

00:12:38.795 --> 00:12:40.630
How did Nike build a brand?

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:42.150
They ran commercials.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.330
They did sponsorship.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:44.893
It's just attention arbitrage.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:47.160
- What does that mean?

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:48.640
- For me it means,

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:49.834
my career,

00:12:49.834 --> 00:12:52.420
oftentimes, I reference Mariano Rivera,

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:53.850
when I talk about my career.
- Mo.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:54.683
- Mo.

00:12:54.683 --> 00:12:57.453
Mo was one of the great
closers in baseball history.

00:12:58.368 --> 00:12:59.201
- Robbie knows that.

00:12:59.201 --> 00:13:00.034
He's a big Yankee fan.

00:13:00.034 --> 00:13:01.970
- And so, you're gonna appreciate this.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:03.290
He had one pitch.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:04.710
He was solid at everything else,

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:05.890
but he had this one pitch.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:08.400
And besides Edgar Martinez,

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:09.963
literally, nobody could hit it.

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:12.490
And that's who I think I am.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:13.930
I'm good at other things,

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.030
I'm good COO, I can operate,

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:17.430
that's why I have businesses.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:18.560
I'm very good at HR.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.040
I'm good at PR.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.290
I'm good at other things.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.500
This is me now talking about myself.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:25.333
If you ask me,

00:13:25.333 --> 00:13:27.600
I think, I'm remarkable at understanding

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.020
what humans are actually doing

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:32.930
before the masses
understand they're doing it.

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:33.763
Right?

00:13:33.763 --> 00:13:36.180
I understood that online dating, in 2001,

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.660
was gonna be mainstream.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:41.170
That wasn't obvious until 2011, right?

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:44.450
Or '13 or, whenever Tinder
really took hold, right?

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:45.283
And so,

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:48.650
what I mean by that is,

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:50.760
I built Wine Library on the back of e-mail

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.630
and Google AdWords.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:54.190
In 1997,

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:56.040
no liquor-store in America,

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:57.090
thought it was a good idea

00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:58.690
to build up an email-newsletter,

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:01.420
versus, the catalog that
they could send in the mail.

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:02.630
Got it?
- Right, yeah.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:06.490
- In 2001, I bought every Google AdWord

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:08.290
for every wine term

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:09.730
and nobody was bidding me up.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:11.268
- Right, got it.
- Got it?

00:14:11.268 --> 00:14:12.101
- Yep.

00:14:12.101 --> 00:14:14.680
- In 2006, this is now
documented, thank God.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:18.310
In 2006, I started a wine show on YouTube

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:20.563
in February, 2006.

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:25.060
I was one of the first people
to move fast on Twitter.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.512
I have podcasts, and...

00:14:27.512 --> 00:14:30.090
I'm consistently first

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.385
and that skill has allowed
me to be good at real-estate.

00:14:33.385 --> 00:14:34.218
- Right.

00:14:34.218 --> 00:14:36.030
- Somebody bought up all of Dumbo.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.403
Somebody once bought up most of Malibu.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.620
Some people see it.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.220
I see human behavior.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:44.600
- Okay, so, what do you see right now?

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:45.610
- Voice.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:46.500
- What does that mean?

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:47.960
- That means that everybody in this room

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.200
and everybody who's watching this video

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:50.460
is gonna do a lot more things

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:52.480
with Alexa and Google Home and Apple Pod

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:54.290
than they think today.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.210
- Like what?

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:58.260
- Order every single food that they eat.

00:14:58.260 --> 00:14:59.390
All of it.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:01.790
In 13 years, you will mouth,

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:03.700
in your office, in your home,

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:06.430
you will say, "Send me a burger."

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:08.280
Or, "Can you get me a Shake Shack?"

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:11.497
Or, "I wanna a Whatchamacallit bar.

00:15:11.497 --> 00:15:12.867
"Get me my Sprite."

00:15:12.867 --> 00:15:13.700
- Quinoa.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:14.533
- That's right.
- Even--

00:15:14.533 --> 00:15:15.460
- I want a quinoa salad.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:17.250
And this is where branding comes in.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.040
I want a quinoa salad.

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:20.660
If you say that to Alexa,

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:22.540
Amazon, now has a lot of leverage.

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:24.920
What quinoa salad are they gonna send you?

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:26.720
Their own private label?

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:30.030
Is some brand gonna pay
$500 to be the affiliate?

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:35.030
So you better say, "I want
Sweet Greens quinoa salad."

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:36.800
Which is why brand

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.320
is about to become so much more...

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:41.490
Brand has always been the most important.

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:43.480
Brand's about to become
even more important

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:47.350
because we're going to an
audio-centric legion world,

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:50.210
not a visual-centric.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:51.043
It's gonna be very big.

00:15:51.043 --> 00:15:53.900
- It sounds like you think
Amazon has a lot of upside here.

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:55.360
- I sure do.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:56.193
- Yeah.

00:15:56.193 --> 00:15:57.310
- Unless the government gets involved,

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:59.380
which, I don't know that stuff.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:00.810
That's above my pay grade.

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:02.940
I would buy Amazon stock in perpetuity

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.670
until Jeff Bezos no
longer runs the company.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:09.450
- But, Amazon, Google, Facebook, Twitter,

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.620
they have a lot of advertising power.

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:12.540
Does that concern you,

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:14.437
that they've got so much of that pie?

00:16:14.437 --> 00:16:15.820
- Of course not,

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:18.720
'cause CBS, ABC and
NBC had that whole pie.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:20.660
Because the New York Times
and the Washington Post

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:22.150
had all that pie.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:23.600
Yahoo at one point.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:25.570
There's always somebody
that controls the pie.

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:27.290
- And always people whining about it.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:28.150
- That's right.

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:29.730
Microsoft should have been broken up.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:31.140
IBM should have been broken up.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:32.818
Google should be broken, now...

00:16:32.818 --> 00:16:35.160
it's the same old game.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.550
- Should they be broken up, Facebook?

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:38.730
- I don't think so.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:40.750
Mainly, because I think that there's

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.160
always another Young Turk,

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:46.680
coming up and she or he is
gonna destroy Mark Zuckerberg

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.730
because Mark Zuckerberg becomes passive.

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:50.790
Because Jeff Bezos becomes passive.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.710
'Cause Bill Gates becomes passive.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.860
Because people become passive.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:55.920
- Right.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:57.530
They become content,

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:58.560
they become--

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:00.850
- Content, old, curious in other things,

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:01.980
different parts of their life.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.560
Sick, die.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:05.000
This is run by people.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:06.660
- Yeah, yeah.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:08.060
So we're in Long Island City,

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.400
we just talked about Amazon.

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:10.233
- Yes.

00:17:10.233 --> 00:17:11.237
- What did you think about that?

00:17:11.237 --> 00:17:13.140
Was the city right to boot them out,

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:14.193
or activists right to boot them out?

00:17:14.193 --> 00:17:15.780
- I am so pumped you asked this question.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.330
- [Andy] Good, let's talk about it.

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:18.193
I have no idea.

00:17:19.151 --> 00:17:20.390
- (laughing) Come on, man.

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:22.350
You said you wanted to talk
about that and you're punting.

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:23.183
- I'm not punting.

00:17:23.183 --> 00:17:24.086
- Okay.

00:17:24.086 --> 00:17:26.590
- The reason, I just got so excited, is,

00:17:26.590 --> 00:17:29.040
as a guy who speaks with heavy conviction,

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:30.640
which could come across as audacity

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:33.130
or ego or overconfidence,

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:35.430
nothing excites me more,
than when I'm interviewed,

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:37.410
when I actually don't know
the answer to something

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:39.370
and I can say, "I don't know."

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:40.203
- But that's just it,

00:17:40.203 --> 00:17:41.036
so why don't you know?

00:17:41.036 --> 00:17:41.869
That's the follow up question.

00:17:41.869 --> 00:17:43.300
- Because I'm not willing
to do the homework of

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:44.580
the collateral things

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:46.850
that happen after the fact, right?

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:49.800
I don't understand, the actual math around

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.100
my intuitive nature is,

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:53.130
when the greatest company in the world

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:54.710
or definitely in America,

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:56.040
wants to set up shop,

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:58.950
that, that has, trickle-down value prop.

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.120
What I don't know,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.020
is the concessions that
the state or the city does,

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:05.800
and the arguments on both
sides of the equation

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:08.668
properly and possibly have

00:18:08.668 --> 00:18:10.740
actual value to them,

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.480
I just haven't taken
the time to dissect it.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:14.850
Let me give you an example.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:16.290
I believe that TV advertising

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:17.740
is grossly over-priced.

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:18.710
- Right.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:20.060
- I also think that the Super Bowl

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:22.870
is the number one best buy in marketing.

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:23.920
So that's a contradiction

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:25.830
within the micro of the macro.

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:27.480
That's why I can't answer to LIC.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:29.373
I don't know.

00:18:29.373 --> 00:18:30.400
- Right, right.

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:32.250
What do you think about

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.910
politicians like Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez,

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:38.010
who says that we should have
more government involved

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:41.170
in terms of people making higher wages?

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:43.152
- You know, it's an interesting question.

00:18:43.152 --> 00:18:45.530
I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:49.640
So I laugh when Americans try to paint

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:52.230
liberals as socialists or communists,

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:55.000
'cause that's Americans that
haven't lived in communism.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:55.833
- [Andy] Right.

00:18:55.833 --> 00:18:57.420
- So here's what I would say,

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:00.433
I am stunningly socially liberal.

00:19:01.352 --> 00:19:03.350
At the same token,

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:06.250
I'm an alpha-entrepreneur

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:08.547
and believe in many of
the things of capitalism.

00:19:08.547 --> 00:19:11.390
What I don't like is
when capitalism gets old

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:12.970
aka an Alpha gets old,

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:14.830
and then she or he
tries to use their money

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:16.470
to change the rules of merit.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.240
So, I'm disgusted by that.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:19.220
At the same token,

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.587
I have many 20-year-old kids
hitting me up and saying,

00:19:21.587 --> 00:19:24.360
"Gary, you're rich, you
should give me your money."

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:26.180
So, I'm very scared of this slippery slope

00:19:26.180 --> 00:19:27.770
of massive entitlement.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:28.603
- Yeah.

00:19:28.603 --> 00:19:29.870
- So,

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:31.370
more government involvement

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:35.900
doesn't hit me well in my
alpha, player-on-the-field,

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:37.760
I'm an entrepreneur.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:39.700
It hits me tremendously well as,

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:40.690
I'm the human being

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:42.420
and I want everybody
to be healthy and happy

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.100
to the best of our abilities.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:46.590
I think the answer is
always somewhere in between.

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:48.830
The biggest problem I have...

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:52.390
listen, I'm willing to
give up 95% of my earning.

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:53.223
I really am.

00:19:53.223 --> 00:19:54.300
Let's go to taxes.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.762
- Maybe getting close
to that in this state.

00:19:55.762 --> 00:19:57.180
- I'm willing to.

00:19:57.180 --> 00:19:58.750
I just wanna be the one who gives it away.

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:00.420
- Mm-hmm, right.

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:01.253
- Wait a minute,

00:20:01.253 --> 00:20:02.086
I'm gonna work my face off,

00:20:02.086 --> 00:20:03.620
bleed in perpetuity,

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:05.460
and I'm gonna give it to you,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:06.860
and you're gonna decide,

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:08.633
who got 36,000 votes?

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:12.800
And you've never operated
anything in your life?

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:14.210
And you're third-generation wealthy

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:15.720
or your ideology,

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:18.000
and you have no practical skills?

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:19.490
This is not about giving away money,

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:20.850
it's about giving away
money to people that

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:22.283
have no idea what to do with it.

00:20:22.283 --> 00:20:24.750
That starts where, it
gets a little complicated.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:28.030
So, I want everybody to be
awesome and happy and healthy,

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:29.817
and I'm willing to pay
unbelievable amounts for it,

00:20:29.817 --> 00:20:32.110
I just wanna see how
you're gonna spend it.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:33.520
- Right, right, right.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:34.527
Shifting gears a little bit--

00:20:34.527 --> 00:20:35.360
- Please.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:37.150
- Everyone wants to be an entrepreneur.

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:39.420
Well maybe not everybody
but a lot of people do

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:42.700
but very few people
actually pull the trigger

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:44.400
and even fewer succeed.

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:46.610
What are the barriers there?

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:47.483
- Talent.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:50.013
- That's disheartening.

00:20:50.013 --> 00:20:51.870
That means you're just...

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.870
you can either play in
the NBA or you can't.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:56.590
- No, that's a very good point.

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.270
I think it's the following.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:00.600
My brother AJ is a better
basketball player than me

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:01.540
'cause he's put in more work

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:03.210
on his left hand and his jumper,

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:04.550
so, he deserves it.

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:06.600
He's not in the NBA.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.600
You can be an entrepreneur,
the problem is,

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:10.620
we've lost sight of, being an entrepreneur

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:12.890
who makes $137,000 a year,

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:14.330
doing what she or he loves,

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:15.730
is an amazing life.

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:16.700
But right now, everybody thinks

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:18.190
they're gonna be Mark Zuckerberg.

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:20.650
So we've become audacious and ludicrous

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:24.740
and delusional in our
entrepreneurial ambitions.

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.430
And so, I think we need a
level of self-awareness.

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:28.860
I think people are trying
to be entrepreneurs

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.510
at a level we've never seen before

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:31.590
'cause the cost of entry

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:32.910
of saying that you're an entrepreneur

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.251
in your Instagram account is very easy.

00:21:35.251 --> 00:21:36.084
- Right.

00:21:36.084 --> 00:21:37.420
- It's also cool right now.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:39.070
I'm actually quite scared about that,

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:40.940
because I don't wanna be the face of that

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:43.230
because I think 95% of
people are gonna fail.

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:45.330
Especially when the economy fails.

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.290
Let me say something straight to camera.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:49.530
If you're not a successful
entrepreneur right now,

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:51.510
in the easiest time to be
a successful entrepreneur

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:53.110
'cause there's so much money in the system

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:54.890
and the internet is that scale,

00:21:54.890 --> 00:21:55.883
you suck.

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:57.800
- All right.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:59.340
That's some tough love right there.

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:00.450
- It's the truth.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:03.520
This is the easiest time,
in the history of life,

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:04.960
to be a successful entrepreneur

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.415
and if you're not and you're struggling,

00:22:07.415 --> 00:22:09.070
then, you're not an entrepreneur.

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:12.590
Listen, I'd love to be playing
for the Jets and the Nicks

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:14.960
or be opening the Grammys.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:16.500
It's fun, it sounds rad.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:18.540
I'm just not capable.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.110
It doesn't mean I can't sing better.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:22.170
It doesn't mean I can't be
a better football player,

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:24.139
backyard, in Central Park.

00:22:24.139 --> 00:22:25.680
You gotta put in the work,

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:28.020
and you have to be
self-aware of your upside.

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:28.853
The other thing is,

00:22:28.853 --> 00:22:29.870
we have too many students

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.410
who now think that
entrepreneurship is cool.

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.700
If you weren't selling
lemonade and baseball-cards

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:37.910
or pogs or burning CDs and
flipping them in school,

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:39.680
if you don't have sales-DNA

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:40.900
or operational-DNA,

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:44.665
or it wasn't fun to play
Store when you were five,

00:22:44.665 --> 00:22:47.630
you didn't gravitate towards it naturally,

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:50.270
you're gravitating towards
it now 'cause it's cool.

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:51.103
- Right.

00:22:51.103 --> 00:22:52.190
And I take it you did all those things.

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:53.980
- All those things.

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:56.313
And so did, so many,

00:22:56.313 --> 00:22:58.270
of the people that are successful

00:22:58.270 --> 00:22:59.563
as entrepreneurs.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:03.610
The same as singers went to choir,

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:06.600
the same as actors signed
up for this Thespian Club

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:07.830
at freshman year.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:08.663
Like it matters.

00:23:08.663 --> 00:23:11.300
The work matters, the talent matters.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:12.340
- What about--

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:14.020
You don't get to wake up one day

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:17.167
and just say, "I'm going to
start the Uber of bananas."

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:20.380
It's just crazy what's going on out there.

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:21.750
Just, the greatest generation of

00:23:21.750 --> 00:23:23.650
fake-entrepreneurship we've ever seen.

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:26.790
- Yeah, that's 'cause of the Internet...

00:23:29.547 --> 00:23:30.380
- 'Cause it's cool.

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:31.213
- It's cultural.

00:23:31.213 --> 00:23:34.670
- If us said, "We're gonna be rappers",

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:36.700
that's cool too but very quickly

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:37.930
we have to prove if we can rap.

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:39.210
I'd be like, "Cool, rap!".

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:40.157
And then, you can't and you're like,

00:23:40.157 --> 00:23:41.410
"You're not a rapper".

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:45.230
I could say, "I'm gonna
be a basketball player".

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:46.550
I'm like, "Cool, show me!".

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:48.290
You can't prove to somebody

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:50.400
in one second that you're
not an entrepreneur.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:51.740
- Right, right.

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:54.210
So, let me ask you about speaking.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:55.300
- Okay.
- You do speaking right?

00:23:55.300 --> 00:23:56.133
- I do.

00:23:56.133 --> 00:23:57.720
- Motivational-speaking you could call it.

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:02.410
Are you concerned, Garry,
that it's manipulative?

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:03.870
- Of course.

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:05.170
- Of course.

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:07.360
- But I don't think I'm manipulative.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:10.390
I think that people wanna
make me a motivational-speaker

00:24:10.390 --> 00:24:11.223
but I'm not.

00:24:11.223 --> 00:24:13.410
If you go watch one hour
of Keynote on YouTube,

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:14.900
listen to what I'm saying.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.843
I'm not talking about The Secret,

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:19.250
just listen to the words.

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:22.830
I'm motivational in my DNA.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:24.670
I'm a rah-rah guy.

00:24:24.670 --> 00:24:26.553
I'd be a great high-school couch.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:29.840
I know, how I come-off

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:33.310
but I ask anybody who wants
to throw that cynicism at me

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:36.860
to go read the transcript
of what I'm saying.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:41.280
I am the most practical,
emigrant, operating person

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:42.940
you will find.

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:45.688
There is no delusion in my optimism.

00:24:45.688 --> 00:24:46.920
- [Andy] Right.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:49.623
- There is no delusion in my optimism.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:52.480
It's funny that you ask me that question,

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:53.970
based on the last six
minutes of this interview

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:54.803
if you think about it, right?

00:24:54.803 --> 00:24:56.220
It makes me happy the way it went.

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:57.897
I don't think the last
six minutes were like,

00:24:57.897 --> 00:25:00.930
"Hey, and I'm gonna manipulate
it, that it's so easy".

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:04.180
I'm desperately, trying to
tell people this is super hard.

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:07.900
I'm equally saying, "How lucky
are we that we can even try?"

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:09.970
Our grandparents couldn't start a company

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:11.173
after-hours on a phone.

00:25:13.684 --> 00:25:16.117
This is the greatest era of the at-bat.

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:19.413
You're not guaranteed to hit a home-run.

00:25:19.413 --> 00:25:20.470
- Right, right.

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.493
- This is greatest era of the at-bat.

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:25.360
That's cool.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:27.610
That makes happy, there's
a lot of people struggling,

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:30.910
paying for college loans and
they get home at 7:00 p.m.

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:31.917
and I'm saying to them,

00:25:31.917 --> 00:25:34.207
"Let me motivate you to not use Netflix

00:25:34.207 --> 00:25:37.977
"and 2K and beer to escape your problems.

00:25:37.977 --> 00:25:40.790
"Let me motivate you at 7 p.m.
to spend the next five hours

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:43.860
to build something for
yourself on Shopify or Ebay

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:48.860
or Instagram or YouTube to get
you out of your situation."

00:25:48.969 --> 00:25:49.802
Got it?

00:25:49.802 --> 00:25:50.635
- Yeah, I got it.

00:25:50.635 --> 00:25:52.144
- It's practical.

00:25:52.144 --> 00:25:56.110
I actually believe that my
natural showmanship-DNA,

00:25:56.110 --> 00:26:01.020
my shtick that is my DNA, grossly
undermines what I'm doing.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:02.860
People think it's why I'm successful.

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:05.380
I think it's undermining my upside.

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:09.390
- 'Cause, people just say
you're all form and no content.

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:11.410
- I literally threw up in
my own mouth when you said

00:26:11.410 --> 00:26:12.920
I was a motivational speaker.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:14.208
- Okay, I'm sorry.

00:26:14.208 --> 00:26:15.041
I didn't want to do that to you.

00:26:15.041 --> 00:26:17.980
- You didn't but I'm telling
you a very important truth.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:18.813
- Yeah, okay.

00:26:18.813 --> 00:26:22.700
- I get it and I understand
that I look that part

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:25.650
but I continue to push people
to listen to what I'm saying.

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:29.930
I'd love to be lower but I'm pump.

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:30.820
- That's who you are.

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:32.833
So, how do you prioritize?

00:26:33.725 --> 00:26:34.940
How do you allocate your time?

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.080
Number one.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:37.641
- My admins rule my life.

00:26:37.641 --> 00:26:40.870
I literally, had no clue we
were doing this just now.

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:41.703
- We're doing it.

00:26:41.703 --> 00:26:42.536
- I'm aware

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.500
She walked over, she's like,
"You ready?", I'm like, "okay."

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:47.960
I didn't even know it was the Yahoo! one.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.470
My calendar rules my life.

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:52.963
Me and my team are thoughtful
about what we book.

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:57.930
I spend the far majority
being the operator of Vayner

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.770
but I do use my personal brand as a

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:02.817
top-of-the-funnel awareness
to drive my business.

00:27:02.817 --> 00:27:05.719
- [Andy] Right.
- Right? So, bring value...

00:27:05.719 --> 00:27:08.170
I'm filming this for my own content,

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:11.080
bringing in value for to
your platform and then

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:13.240
the serendipity of somebody
becoming aware of me,

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:15.020
digging-in then looking.

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:18.120
So, I do use my personal
brand, as a top-of-the-funnel,

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.860
business-development-tool
for my businesses.

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:22.620
- Where do you wanna take this?

00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:23.937
It's the old, "Where do
you want to see yourself

00:27:23.937 --> 00:27:25.020
"20 years from now Gary?".

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:25.870
But, I mean, it's important.

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:27.120
- No, no, it's very important.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:30.950
I like being thoughtful of
what I'm doing professionally.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:32.930
The two things that I'm most excited about

00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:36.470
are the continuous
journey, of the ambition,

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:38.650
of trying to buy the New York Jets.

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:40.380
Which is my absolute professional goal.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:41.350
- Really?
- That's right.

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:42.870
- Are you serious about that?

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:44.053
- As a heart-attack.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.090
Yeah, it's been my goal for my whole life.

00:27:47.090 --> 00:27:48.970
- [Andy] Do the current owners know this?

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:49.900
Are they aware of this?

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:51.070
- You know, it's funny,

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.080
I'm not sure and I have no
interest in wasting their time

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:55.150
because I'm not, anywhere close yet,

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:57.283
to the financial level that I need to be.

00:27:58.180 --> 00:27:59.013
Probably do.

00:27:59.013 --> 00:28:00.470
I put it out there quite a bit

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:05.400
and my path to that is
to build Vayner Media,

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.940
to build the greatest
marketing engine in the world,

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:11.610
to then buy historic brands,
when the economy collapses.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:14.030
So, I wanna buy Tootsie Rolls or

00:28:15.500 --> 00:28:16.745
Reebok or...

00:28:16.745 --> 00:28:17.578
- [Andy] Right.

00:28:17.578 --> 00:28:18.530
- And then I wanna become the CEO of that.

00:28:18.530 --> 00:28:20.340
So, I wanna buy something
in the next decade

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:21.980
for 2 hundred-million
to 5 hundred-million,

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:23.900
then, I want to run it for a decade

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:26.200
and then flip it, for
3 billion to 5 billion

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:27.770
and buy the Jets

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:31.560
and then, I wanna make content
that leaves a positive legacy

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:36.560
in allowing people to, at least,
use me as a context point.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:38.410
This is how I'm doing it.

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:39.770
Take what works for you

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:43.815
and place some legacy on the content.

00:28:43.815 --> 00:28:48.630
- Wow, that is well thought-out
and clearly defined.

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:49.970
- I appreciate it.

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:51.980
- How do you see yourself,

00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:55.533
using your influence on the world?

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:58.473
It's kind of at your core.

00:28:59.390 --> 00:29:02.100
- I believe that in 40
years, people will realize,

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.514
during this period of time,

00:29:03.514 --> 00:29:06.880
that, I was one of the
few unique individuals,

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:08.790
who had the ability penetrate

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:11.480
15 to 25 year old, young men

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.230
and was able to re-frame their mind

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:17.100
of not being as, into
watches and private jets

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:19.170
and models and cash

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:20.990
but made them start respecting things

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:25.310
like patience and gratitude
and empathy and kindness

00:29:25.310 --> 00:29:28.430
and that I will be far-more
revered, after I'm gone,

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:30.190
for doing it, then I'll ever be

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:31.490
while I'm around doing it.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:34.810
- Garry Vaynerchuck, thanks so much

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:36.800
for talking with us today and
thanks for having us here.

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:37.633
- Thank you.

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:42.600
- I'm Andy Serwer, you've
been watching Influencers.

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:43.800
We'll see you next time.

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:50.254
Great, that was super-cool, I loved it.

00:29:50.254 --> 00:29:51.488
- [Gary] Thank you, thank you.
- Really, really, interesting.

00:29:51.488 --> 00:29:52.488
- [Andy] I loved it.

